    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 4\n    nc = 11\n    facility_coords = [(47, 0), (84, 25), (29, 42), (3, 66)]\n    customer_coords = [(12, 21), (17, 60), (57, 5), (76, 30), (68, 92), (15, 85), (17, 72), (3, 35), (95, 77), (76, 2), (90, 11)]\n    capacities = [93.0, 132.0, 155.0, 171.0]\n    demands = [48.0, 10.0, 42.0, 19.0, 14.0, 21.0, 48.0, 34.0, 45.0, 22.0, 10.0]\n    fixed_costs = [217.0, 476.0, 436.0, 282.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: min throughput 23 per open facility\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) >= 23.0*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: F3 requires F2\n    m.addConstr(open_f[3] <= open_f[2])\n\n    # Policy: opening budget cap $1597\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 1597.0)\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Warehouse Activation and Customer Fulfillment Network Decision\n\nOverview\nWe are preparing a distribution network decision that determines which candidate warehouses to activate and how to route shipments from open sites to customers. The objective is to meet all customer demand while minimizing the combined spend on facility activation and transportation. Activation involves a fixed cost per site, and transportation spend scales with shipment distance and volume at the published rate. The locations of candidate facilities and customers are specified by map coordinates, and each facility has a defined handling capacity.\n\nBusiness Requirements\n- Customer coverage. Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied for the planning period. Where it reduces overall cost or supports capacity balancing, a customer\u2019s demand may be allocated across multiple open facilities.\n- Facility activation and shipping. A facility may ship product only if it is activated. Each activation carries a fixed cost. Total shipments assigned to any open facility must not exceed its handling capacity.\n- Minimum utilization threshold. To justify operating overhead, every opened facility must handle at least 23 units of total outbound shipments. Any site that cannot reach this volume should remain closed.\n- Dependency between candidate sites. The candidate site designated F3 at coordinates (3, 66) depends on the regional hub at F2 at coordinates (29, 42). If F3 is activated, F2 must also be activated in the same plan.\n- Capital guardrail for activations. The capital budget for facility activation is capped at $1597. The sum of fixed activation costs for all open facilities must not exceed this amount.\n- Transportation cost basis. Shipping costs are driven by distance and volume moved at the established transport rate. Distances are derived from the map coordinates of the shipping facility and receiving customer. The network decision will evaluate the trade\u2011off between opening additional sites (to shorten hauls) and consolidating volume into fewer sites (to avoid activation spend).\n- Decision goal. Among all feasible configurations that follow the above policies and operating limits, we will recommend the set of facilities to open and the customer\u2011to\u2011facility shipment plan that delivers the lowest total cost (fixed activation plus transportation).\n\nDecision Approach and Deliverables\n- Evaluate candidate activation combinations that fit within the activation budget, satisfy the minimum utilization threshold for each opened site, and respect facility capacity limits while meeting all customer demand.\n- For each feasible activation set, assign customer volumes to open facilities in a way that minimizes total transportation spend, subject to capacity and policy requirements.\n- Deliver a recommended configuration including: facilities to activate; customer\u2011to\u2011facility shipment allocations; facility throughput and utilization; total estimated fixed activation spend and transportation spend; and sensitivity views showing how the recommendation shifts under alternative budgets or throughput thresholds.\n- Provide an implementation checklist covering commercial approvals for activation spend, operational ramp\u2011up sequencing at selected facilities, and data handoffs to planning and transportation management.\n\nAnnex: Data Inputs\n- Candidate facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (47, 0)\n  - F1: (84, 25)\n  - F2: (29, 42)\n  - F3: (3, 66)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (12, 21)\n  - C1: (17, 60)\n  - C2: (57, 5)\n  - C3: (76, 30)\n  - C4: (68, 92)\n  - C5: (15, 85)\n  - C6: (17, 72)\n  - C7: (3, 35)\n  - C8: (95, 77)\n  - C9: (76, 2)\n  - C10: (90, 11)\n- Facility capacities (units per period): [93.0, 132.0, 155.0, 171.0]\n- Customer demand (units per period): [48.0, 10.0, 42.0, 19.0, 14.0, 21.0, 48.0, 34.0, 45.0, 22.0, 10.0]\n- Fixed activation costs by facility: [217.0, 476.0, 436.0, 282.0]\n- Transport rate (per unit per km): 0.5",
